  ],
  "details": "<p>Six inscribed paving slabs were found in a wing of Sargon II\u2019s palace that was mistakenly identified as a harem and those in-situ texts helped identify that part of the building as the palace\u2019s religious quarter. The temple sector of D\u016br-\u0160arruk\u012bn (modern Khorsabad) comprised temples dedicated to the deities Adad, Ea (Nin\u0161iku), Ningal, Ninurta, S\u00een, and \u0160ama\u0161. The single-room temple of the god Adad, which was accessed from Courtyard XXVII, was identified from the eight-line Akkadian inscription (Frame, RINAP 2 online <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/rinap/rinap2/Q006500\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\"><span>Sargon II </span>19</a>) discovered in the doorway of Room 166. The ancient name of the temple is not known.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "As part of the palatial complex of D\u016br-\u0160arruk\u012bn (modern Khorsabad), the eighth-century-BC Assyrian king Sargon II (r. 721\u2013705 BC) constructed six temples, including one to the god Adad.",
